  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday through Tuesday)\nIssued at 144 PM MDT Wed Jul 29 2026\n\nFriday starts a more active few days of storms across the area. \nRemnant outflow boundaries are likely to be in place across eastern \nNM, and the high pressure looks to be shoved to the west by an \napproaching trough across the central Great Plains. As such, with \nreturn flow across eastern NM throughout the day, upslope flow in \nconjuncture with northwest to north upper level flow sets the stage \nfor numerous storms along the central mountain chain. Given the \nforecast increase of bulk shear values, there is higher confidence \nthat a few severe storms will be observed across northeast and east \ncentral NM. Additionally, with the potential for repeated rounds of \nstorms by either training or redevelopment, there is concern for \nflash flooding as well. For the most part, many eastern NM locations \nhave not seen much rainfall over the past several weeks, so this \nrainfall is sure to be welcomed. However, cannot rule out \"too much \nof a good thing\" rainfall occurring in some of these dry locations. \nOn top of this, the more north-south storm motion sets up a higher \nrisk of burn scar flash flooding over the Ruidoso area. One last \nitem for Friday: clusters of storms are likely to produce an outflow \nboundary surging westward towards the central mountain chain, and \nlooks increasingly likely to generate a gusty east canyon wind for \nthe ABQ and Santa Fe metro area, as well as areas down the Rio \nGrande Valley.\n\nSaturday sets up similarly to Friday, though the high pressure \ncontinues to push westward. Depending on how far west that goes, it \nwill dictate what parts of the state would see the highest storm \ncoverage. Currently, the most likely scenario is another round of \nnumerous storms developing over the central mountain chain pushing \nsouthward. Forecast bulk shear values are sufficient enough for a \nfew storms to turn strong to severe once again. Given this moisture \nis most likely to be channeled along the central mountain chain, \nthis looks like another elevated risk day for flash flooding across \nthe Ruidoso area burn scars. Conditional flooding threats may also \ndevelop elsewhere, but would depend on the rainfall observed on \nFriday.\n\nThe high looks to rebound back towards NM on Sunday, orienting the \nflow over the state in a more northeast-southwest direction. This \nlikely heavily limits the storm coverage across the area to only a \nfew scattered storms across the high terrain. Storm chances look to \ngradually rebound through next week as the high is favored to break \ndown a bit and for moisture to begin wrapping around. Hotter \ntemperatures also look to favor central and western NM, where the \nlower storm coverage and higher pressure heights are forecast to be.",
